// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Normal Distribution: Critical Z-values and invNorm areas ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
loadlibrary("stats");

// Part 1: Right-tail area to left-tail area conversion
// Using a fixed set of common tail areas to ensure stability and clarity.
$rightArea = randfrom(array(0.01, 0.025, 0.05, 0.10));
$leftArea = 1 - $rightArea;

// Part 2: Critical z for that area
// Standard normal (mean 0, sd 1)
$zCrit = invnormalcdf($leftArea, 0, 1);
$zCritRounded = round($zCrit, 3);

// Part 3: Middle percentage
$midPct = randfrom(array(90, 95, 98, 99));
$midArea = $midPct / 100;
$tailArea = (1 - $midArea) / 2;
$zMid = invnormalcdf($tailArea, 0, 1);
$zMidRounded = round($zMid, 3);

$anstypes = "number,number,number";
$answer[0] = $leftArea;
$answer[1] = $zCritRounded;
$answer[2] = $zMidRounded;

// Solution guide construction
$sol0 = "To find the z-score for a right-tail area of $rightArea, we must provide the area to the left. The left area is `1 - $rightArea = $leftArea`.";
$sol1 = "Using the invNorm function with a left area of $leftArea, we find `z = invNorm($leftArea, 0, 1) \approx $zCritRounded`.";
$sol2 = "For the middle $midPct%, the remaining area is `1 - " . $midArea . " = " . (1 - $midArea) . "`. This is split between two tails, so each tail has an area of " . round($tailArea, 4) . ". The z-score bounding the left tail is `invNorm(" . round($tailArea, 4) . ", 0, 1) \approx $zMidRounded`.";

$solutionguide = "Part (a): $sol0\nPart (b): $sol1\nPart (c): $sol2";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
Consider a standard normal distribution (mean `\mu = 0` and standard deviation `\sigma = 1`).

<p>(a) You want to find the critical z-score that leaves an area of $rightArea in the right tail. Since the invNorm function requires the area to the left, what area should you enter?
<br />Area to the left = $answerbox[0]</p>

<p>(b) Using the area from part (a), find the critical z-score. Round your answer to three decimal places.
<br />`z =` $answerbox[1]</p>

<p>(c) The middle $midPct% of the standard normal curve lies between `-z` and `z`. Find the value of the lower (negative) z-score. Round your answer to three decimal places.
<br />`z =` $answerbox[2]</p>

